        },
        "elapsedMs": 14771,
        "text": "Since you’ve been enjoying **Premiere Pro** and learning its **Lumetri Color panel**, I’d start with resources that build on those skills:\n\n### Free tutorials\n- **[Adobe Premiere tutorials](https://helpx.adobe.com/premiere-pro/tutorials.html)** — A reliable starting point for organizing footage, trimming clips, working with audio, and exporting. Look for lessons on Lumetri scopes and color correction, too.\n- **[Premiere Gal](https://www.youtube.com/@PremiereGal)** — Practical Premiere walkthroughs covering captions, audio cleanup, effects, and editing workflows.\n- **[Justin Odisho](https://www.youtube.com/@JustinOdisho)** — Short, focused tutorials for learning a particular Premiere technique or solving an editing problem.\n- **[This Guy Edits](https://www.youtube.com/@ThisGuyEdits)** — Especially useful for the *creative* side: choosing takes, shaping emotion, pacing, and knowing when to cut.\n\n### Structured courses\n- **[LinkedIn Learning](https://www.linkedin.com/learning/)** — Search for *Premiere Pro Essential Training*. It’s a good option for a step-by-step foundation; some public libraries provide free access.\n- **[Inside the Edit](https://www.insidetheedit.com/)** — Paid training focused on storytelling and editorial decisions rather than just software controls.\n\n### A book worth reading\n- **_In the Blink of an Eye_ by Walter Murch** — A short, thoughtful book about why cuts work and how editing guides an audience’s attention and emotions. Useful regardless of software.\n\n**A manageable way to start:** Make a 30–60-second video from your own footage. First concentrate on cuts and pacing, then clean up the audio, and finally use Lumetri to balance exposure and white balance. Follow one relevant tutorial at each stage rather than trying to learn everything at once."
      },
